    <string name="ra_site_wari">Wari Archaeological Complex</string>
    <string name="ra_site_quinua">Pampa de la Quinua</string>
    <string name="ra_site_vilcas">Vilcashuamán</string>
    <string name="ra_wari_narration">Welcome to the Wari Archaeological Complex, the ancient capital of the Wari Empire, which flourished between the seventh and thirteenth centuries. In front of you is the reconstruction of one of its enclosures. In sectors such as Monjachayuq and Cheqo Wasi, the stone walls reached up to twelve meters high, and an advanced canal system supplied the city. Move the slider to compare how it looks today with how it may have looked at its peak.</string>
    <string name="ra_wari_poi1_title">High-walled enclosure</string>
    <string name="ra_wari_poi1_desc">This is how it may have looked in Wari times: stone walls up to 12 meters high around the space.</string>
    <string name="ra_wari_poi2_title">Water canals</string>
    <string name="ra_wari_poi2_desc">The Wari built an advanced canal system to bring water to the city.</string>
    <string name="ra_quinua_narration">You are at the Pampa de la Quinua Historic Sanctuary. Here, on December ninth, eighteen twenty-four, the patriot troops of Marshal Antonio José de Sucre defeated the royalist army in the last great battle of Spanish American independence. The forty-four-meter commemorative obelisk was inaugurated in nineteen seventy-four for the battle\'s one hundred and fiftieth anniversary.</string>
    <string name="ra_quinua_poi1_title">44 m obelisk</string>
    <string name="ra_quinua_poi1_desc">Inaugurated in 1974 for the 150th anniversary of the Battle of Ayacucho. You can climb it to see the plain.</string>
    <string name="ra_quinua_poi2_title">Battlefield</string>
    <string name="ra_quinua_poi2_desc">On December 9, 1824, the patriot and royalist armies fought here.</string>
    <string name="ra_vilcas_narration">Welcome to Vilcashuamán, which in Quechua means Sacred Falcon. It was one of the most important administrative centers of Tahuantinsuyo, founded by the Inca Pachacútec in the fifteenth century. In front of you is the reconstruction of the Ushno, the five-platform ceremonial pyramid with the Inca\'s throne at the top. Nearby is the Temple of the Sun and the Moon, which was turned into a colonial church.</string>
    <string name="ra_vilcas_poi1_title">The Inca\'s throne</string>
    <string name="ra_vilcas_poi1_desc">At the top of the Ushno is the stone seat from which the Inca presided over ceremonies.</string>
    <string name="ra_vilcas_poi2_title">Five platforms</string>
    <string name="ra_vilcas_poi2_desc">The Ushno is a stepped pyramid with five levels.</string>
